Serializing Lottery Numbers

Hi Askmath, hope this is a good one for you. I have an interesting  programming problem where in I have a need to convert a set of lottery  numbers into a single, serialized number. So for instance, imagine a  game where you have 6 balls drawn, if the numbers picked or drawn were  1-2-3-4-5-6, that would be represented as a '1'. If it was 1-2-3-4-5-7,  that would be '2', if it was 1-2-3-4-5-6-8, thats number 3, and so on...  Thusly, if lets say there were 10 possible numbers that could be drawn  for any given ball, the LAST possible number in the drawing would be  5-6-7-8-9-10, and that would be serial number...whatever the last  possible number in that series would be... I dunno, like 151200, I  think?

&amp;#x200B;

So I'm trying to come up with a function of sorts which can do a math to convert x1, x2, x3, x4, x5, x6 to that single serial number, for any given combination of balls and numbers. Easy to do for one ball, f(x) = x. For two balls, I think it'd be more like f(x1, x2) = (x1 \* x2 - 1)... I don't know that this same formula would work indefinitely though thusly that f(xn) = (xn \* xn-1 \* xn-2 ...) - n. Can anyone here think of a more simplistic mathematical way to express this problem for an unlimited number of balls and numbers?

&amp;#x200B;

edit: One reason I'm asking this here instead of a programming reddit, is I already have a programming solution that I don't like, which involves taking the numbers, say 1-64, creating an array of binary numbers that many digits long, and for each ball, turning that digit to a 1, and then converting that long binary number into a square graphic, which gets into a long conversation about what the program does, which is not really beneficial to this problem at hand. I'm looking for a more pure mathematical solution than a programming one.

