Real-life system of equations help?

I will preface this with saying that I'm not a student, though I imagine I'm a math teacher's dream right now as an adult in the workplace struggling to create a formula to solve an actual day-to-day problem. 

&amp;#x200B;

I work in a manufacturing job. We make small batches of products that we put into bottles for retail sale. We have different types of products which may be sold in several size options, and the size of the batch varies by product type. Up to this point, production has involved a lot of best-guesses, but we're really trying to tighten up our process with more robust inventory and sales data. For a given batch, we need to know how many of each size of product to make while also taking into account what we have in existing stock. Our primary goals are to not run out of one size prematurely or have a gross overstock of any individual size. 

&amp;#x200B;

For a couple of examples of numbers to get a feel for what we're working with: For product X, a batch yields \~560oz. Product X is available as an 8oz bottle or a 1oz bottle. We sell approx. 100 8oz to 70 1oz. We currently have 10 8oz and 30 1oz in stock. When we fill the next batch of this product, how many 8oz and how many 1oz bottles should we make? 

